#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163793 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f3ef3be7-aacb-5053-aed6-a050c2e08656" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163794 #edb/ref 1080 "0.562" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163794 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163794 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ca4ad11-a9a2-5e9d-b942-28716e357410" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163795 #edb/ref 1080 "0.682" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163795 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163795 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2451e569-ab1f-53fd-b6b7-a4e2c0b10203" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163796 #edb/ref 1080 "0.717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163796 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163796 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4280b356-4cc6-5e95-8643-cd0889d3d58e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163797 #edb/ref 1080 "0.697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163797 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163797 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7c305fd3-da6a-5509-82b5-27016b5d41be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166482 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10418" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166482 #edb/ref 1071 "A travel agency offers three itineraries, itinerary $1,$ itinerary $2,$ and $3.$ For each itinerary, customers can choose to travel using their own car or the agency's bus.\n\n- Itinerary $1$ is chosen by $25\\%$ of the customers, and $60\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\n- Itinerary $2$ is chosen by $30\\%$ of the customers, and $30\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\n- Itinerary $3$ is chosen by $45\\%$ of the customers, and $20\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\nIf $65\\%$ of all customers travel with children, and $40\\%$ of customers with children choose the bus, what is the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses the bus travels with children? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166482 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc694d40-d150-55e6-981f-0ca6b5d91e21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166482 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be the event that a customer travels with children, $B$ be the event that a customer takes the bus, and $I_1, I_2, I_3$ be the event that a customer chooses itinerary $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({I}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(B|{I}_{1})=0.6 \\\\  & P({I}_{2})=0.3 &  & P(B|{I}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P({I}_{3})=0.45 &  & P(B|{I}_{3})=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $I_1 \\sqcup I_2  \\sqcup I_3$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(I_1)+ P(I_2) + P(I_3) = 0.25+0.3+0.45 = 1.\n$$\n\n- Since $65\\%$ of customers have children, we have\n\n$$\nP(C) = 0.65.\n$$\n\n- Since $40\\%$ of customers with children choose the bus, we have\n\n$$\nP(B | C) = 0.4.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses the bus travels with children, which is represented as $P(C | B).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C|B) & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B|{I}_{1})P({I}_{1})+P(B|{I}_{2})P({I}_{2})+P(B|{I}_{3})P({I}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.4)(0.65)}{(0.6)(0.25)+(0.3)(0.3)+(0.2)(0.45)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.26}{0.33} \\\\  & ≈0.788,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166697 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8911" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166697 #edb/ref 1071 "The disjoint union $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup A_3$ of events $A_1,A_2,A_3$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P(A_1|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.23, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.26, & P({A}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.18, & P({A}_{3})=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166697 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "699d9c0c-bb98-5710-8d98-10b6f35ab202" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166697 #edb/ref 1074 "If the disjoint union $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup \\ldots \\sqcup A_n$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{P(B)} = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{\\sum_{i=1}^n P(B|A_i)P(A_i)}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,\\ldots,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup A_3$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP(A_1) + P(A_2) + P(A_3) = 1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}) & =1-(P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})) \\\\  & =1-(0.3+0.4) \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.23)(0.3)}{(0.23)(0.3)+(0.26)(0.3)+(0.18)(0.4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.069}{0.219} \\\\  & ≈0.315.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166698 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166698 #edb/ref 1071 "Potatoes produced by a particular farm come in three different varieties, variety $1,$ variety $2,$ and variety $3.$\n\n- $36\\%$ of the potatoes are of variety $1,$ and $20\\%$ of those potatoes weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g.$\n\n- $17\\%$ of the potatoes are of variety $2,$ and $6\\%$ of those potatoes weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g.$\n\n- $47\\%$ of the potatoes are of the variety $3.$\n\nIf $16\\%$ of potatoes from the entire farm are sold to a local restaurant, $40\\%$ of potatoes sold to the restaurant weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g,$ and $15\\%$ of potatoes that weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g$ are sold to the restaurant, what is the probability that a randomly selected potato from variety $3$ weighs more than $100\\,\\mathrm g?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166698 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "284f5029-19eb-53d4-a3b7-0f66d33b9cc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166698 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $R$ be the event that a potato is sold to the restaurant, $W$ be the event that a potato weighs more than $100\\,\\mathrm g,$ and $V_1, V_2, V_3$ be the event that a potato is of variety $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({V}_{1})=0.36 &  & P(W|{V}_{1})=0.2 \\\\  & P({V}_{2})=0.17 &  & P(W|{V}_{2})=0.06 \\\\  & P({V}_{3})=0.47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $V_1 \\sqcup V_2  \\sqcup V_3$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(V_1)+ P(V_2) + P(V_3) = 0.36+0.17+0.47 = 1.\n$$\n\n- Since $16\\%$ of potatoes from the entire farm are sold to a local restaurant, we have\n\n$$\nP(R) = 0.16.\n$$\n\n- Since $40\\%$ potatoes sold to the restaurant weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(W | R) = 0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since $15\\%$ of potatoes that weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g$ are sold to the restaurant, we have\n\n$$\nP(R | W) = 0.15.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen potato of variety $3$ weighs more than $100\\,\\mathrm g,$ which is represented as $P(W | V_3).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|W) & =\\frac{P(W|R)P(R)}{P(W)} \\\\ P(R|W) & =\\frac{P(W|R)P(R)}{P(W|{V}_{1})P({V}_{1})+P(W|{V}_{2})P({V}_{2})+P(W|{V}_{3})P({V}_{3})} \\\\ 0.15 & =\\frac{(0.4)(0.16)}{(0.2)(0.36)+(0.06)(0.17)+P(W|{V}_{3})(0.47)} \\\\ 0.15 & =\\frac{0.064}{0.0822+0.47P(W|{V}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(W | V_3),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.15(0.0822+0.47P(W|{V}_{3})) & =0.064 \\\\ 0.0822+0.47P(W|{V}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.064}{0.15} \\\\ 0.0822+0.47P(W|{V}_{3}) & =\\frac{32}{75} \\\\ 0.47P(W|{V}_{3}) & =\\frac{32}{75}-0.0822 \\\\ P(W|{V}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.47}(\\frac{32}{75}-0.0822) \\\\ P(W|{V}_{3}) & ≈0.733\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166847 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166847 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166847 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166847 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166847 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166847 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166847 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166847 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166847 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Conditional Probability Using Bayes' Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166847 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166847 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e9d77603-c824-5160-80fc-3d10353fe243" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166848 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166848 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166848 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1045 "Extending Bayes' Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186167781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b36ed83a-cc37-5ff2-a68e-2a0e28c0b24e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167116 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167116 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147132" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167116 #edb/ref 1071 "The disjoint union $A_{1}⊔A_{2}⊔A_{3}$ of events $A_{1}, A_{2}, A_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P(A_{3} \\mid B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$P(B\\begin{vmatrix}A_{1}) = 0.5, \\\\ P(B \\mid A_{2}) = 0.6,\\end{vmatrix}P(A_{2}) = 0.2; P(B \\mid A_{3}) = 0.1, \\mid P(A_{3}) = 0.7$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167116 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20ddccd9-19cf-5781-a2f6-c8e6f37cdb70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167116 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167117 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167117 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147235" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167117 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167117 #edb/ref 1071 "The students in a particular school are split among three classes, class $1,$ class $2,$ and class $3.$\n\n- Class $1$ contains $25%$ of the students in the school, and $55%$ of those students take biology.\n\n- Class $2$ contains $35%$ of the students in the school, and $40%$ of those students take biology.\n\n- Class $3$ contains $40%$ of the students in the school.\n\nIf $45%$ of students in the entire school are female, $80%$ of female students take biology, and $70%$ of biology students are female, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from class $3$ takes biology? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167117 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e39e7e2c-b39e-5302-b92e-8f13f6ffd98c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167117 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167117 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $B$ be the event that a student takes biology, and ${C}_{1},{C}_{2},{C}_{3}$ be the event that a student is in class $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({C}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(B|{C}_{1})=0.55 \\\\  & P({C}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(B|{C}_{2})=0.4 \\\\  & P({C}_{3})=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${C}_{1}⊔{C}_{2}⊔{C}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({C}_{1})+P({C}_{2})+P({C}_{3})=0.25+0.35+0.4=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $45%$ of students in the entire school are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since $80%$ of females take biology, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|F)=0.8.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of biology students are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|B)=0.7.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student from class $3$ takes biology, which is represented as $P(B|{C}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B)} \\\\ P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3})} \\\\ 0.7 & =\\frac{(0.8)(0.45)}{(0.55)(0.25)+(0.4)(0.35)+P(B|{C}_{3})(0.4)} \\\\ 0.7 & =\\frac{0.36}{0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(B|{C}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.7(0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3})) & =0.36 \\\\ 0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.36}{0.7} \\\\ 0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{18}{35} \\\\ 0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{18}{35}-0.2775 \\\\ P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.4}(\\frac{18}{35}-0.2775) \\\\ P(B|{C}_{3}) & ≈0.592\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167118 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167118 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148673" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167118 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167118 #edb/ref 1071 "Arthur and Sara are finding the best recipe for chocolate muffins, and they test three different oven cooking modes, mode $1,$ mode $2,$ and mode $3.$ They also try two types of yeast, A and B.\n\n- They use mode $1$ to cook the $30%$ of their muffins, and $90%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\n- They use mode $2$ to cook the $40%$ of their muffins, and $85%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\n- They use mode $3$ to cook the $30%$ of their muffins, and $88%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\nIf $20%$ of all muffins was prepared with yeast A, and $87%$ of yeast A muffins turn out well, what is the probability that a randomly chosen good muffin is done with yeast A? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167118 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dea495bf-07d9-54cd-baea-bb953539d3dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167118 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167118 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the event that a muffin is made with yeast A, $G$ be the event that a muffin turns out well, and ${M}_{1},{M}_{2},{M}_{3}$ be the event that a muffin is cooked with mode $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({M}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(G|{M}_{1})=0.9 \\\\  & P({M}_{2})=0.4 &  & P(G|{M}_{2})=0.85 \\\\  & P({M}_{3})=0.3 &  & P(G|{M}_{3})=0.88\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${M}_{1}⊔{M}_{2}⊔{M}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({M}_{1})+P({M}_{2})+P({M}_{3})=0.3+0.4+0.3=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of muffins are made with yeast A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.2.\n$$\n\n- Since $87%$ of muffins made with yeast A turn out well, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=0.87.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen good muffin is made with yeast A, which is represented as $P(A|G).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|G) & =\\frac{P(G|A)P(A)}{P(G)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(G|A)P(A)}{P(G|{M}_{1})P({M}_{1})+P(G|{M}_{2})P({M}_{2})+P(G|{M}_{3})P({M}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.87)(0.2)}{(0.9)(0.3)+(0.85)(0.4)+(0.88)(0.3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.174}{0.874} \\\\  & ≈0.199,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167119 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167119 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167119 #edb/ref 1071 "Arthur and Sara are finding the best recipe for chocolate muffins, and they test three different oven cooking modes, mode $1$, mode $2$, and mode $3$. They also try two types of yeast, A and B.\n\n- They use mode $1$ to cook the $45%$ of their muffins, and $80%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n- They use mode $2$ to cook the $35%$ of their muffins.\n- They use mode $3$ to cook the $20%$ of their muffins, and $90%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\nIf $65%$ of all muffins were prepared with yeast A, $82%$ of yeast A muffins turn out well, and $68%$ of good muffins were prepared with yeast A, what is the probability that a randomly chosen muffin cooked with mode $2$ turned out well? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167119 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b92de6f-2dd5-5916-8078-94243b8290b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167119 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167713 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167713 #edb/ref 1061 33287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167713 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e6025e7-7c05-542d-a78a-f9d7e627be29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167713 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167714 #edb/ref 1061 33288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167714 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd5da403-f379-5dee-9a50-ecafcf083c36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167714 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167772 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167772 #edb/ref 1061 39256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167772 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7a97606a-98ac-50e6-9e3c-78ae18820b2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167772 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167781 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167781 #edb/ref 1061 42168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167781 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b6bd91e8-6598-5c56-9fa4-bf1144fc830d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167781 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168143 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we will generalize Bayes' theorem to deal with situations where the sample space $\\mathcal S$ is partitioned into a disjoint union of multiple events. \n\nSuppose we have three events $A_1,A_2,$ and $A_3$ that form a disjoint union of the sample space $\\mathcal S,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/89/89a78ef526fcd29a59bacd4d6ae1a456a596d6616af7453f1ba530a610d9a2ac.png)\n\n \n\nNow consider an event $B$ that also lies in the sample space:\n\n![](images/da/daa96423e45f9f80897e2c2d61177eab2faff77f440921866d1b81b7ef2cfe09.png)\n\n \n\nBayes' theorem states that\n\n$$\nP(A_1|B)=\\dfrac{P(B|A_1)P(A_1)}{P(B)}.\n$$\n\nNow, the law of total probability tells us that\n\n$$\nP(B)=P(B|A_1)P(A_1) + P(B|A_2)P(A_2) + P(B|A_3)P(A_3).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Bayes' theorem, we get\n\n$$\nP(A_1 | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_1) P(A_1)}{P(B|A_1)P(A_1) + P(B|A_2)P(A_2) + P(B|A_3)P(A_3)} .\n$$\n\nUsing similar arguments, we can say that for $A_k$ for $k=1,2,3,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{P(B|A_1)P(A_1) + P(B|A_2)P(A_2) + P(B|A_3)P(A_3)}\n$$\n\nwhich is usually written more compactly as\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{\\sum_{i=1}^3 P(B|A_i)P(A_i)}.\n$$\n\nIt's easy to see how this generalizes. If the disjoint union $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup \\ldots \\sqcup A_n$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{\\sum_{i=1}^n P(B|A_i)P(A_i)}\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to apply this theorem." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168143 #edb/ref 1065 6969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168143 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bf17685f-9890-54cc-b8a2-15815558e87a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168143 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486603 #edb/ref 1080 "0.463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486603 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486603 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05268a54-1ca3-5236-8a32-72d18477c132" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486610 #edb/ref 1080 "0.475" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486610 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486610 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08940ee3-c31c-52b4-8370-33f3d3aa8657" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486615 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{20}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486615 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486615 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0adaca2a-25b8-5492-8e73-6d48d5c107c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486638 #edb/ref 1080 "0.115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486638 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486638 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "13b28de4-aa80-5f7f-a8f5-b4f77a28ca63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486656 #edb/ref 1080 "0.623" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486656 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486656 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1faf867c-bba7-5f95-927b-c813a9e9dcf0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486669 #edb/ref 1080 "0.354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486669 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486669 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2663f13b-e38f-5407-950a-5fe65e600172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486692 #edb/ref 1080 "0.325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486692 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486692 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2fa3b441-f3be-5a7b-8222-c944862c87a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486719 #edb/ref 1080 "0.65" #edb/ref 17592186045